00:14:10.589 --> 00:14:12.383
aren't going to matter in the future.

273
00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.297
...on lunch break and you make a cup of tea. and...

274
00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:49.360
Speeches!

275
00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:51.000
Speeches. everybody!

276
00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:56.150
Hey. guys.

277
00:14:56.236 --> 00:14:59.363
I just want to welcome you to Morgan and
Lorna's 50th wedding anniversary.

278
00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:03.500
I know some of you have come a long way.

279
00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:08.040
Some from Victoria. New South Wales.
Jim and Kate. coming down from Townsville.

280
00:15:08.073 --> 00:15:09.073
the three-day drive.

281
00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:10.100
Thank you.

282
00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.466
All the rest of you
drove about five or ten minutes

283
00:15:12.552 --> 00:15:14.301
which is not nearly as impressive.

284
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:17.060
But I. myself. just wandered up from
underneath the house.

285
00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:23.160
When I was born. Morgan and Lorna
had already been married for 16 years.

286
00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:25.560
And they've been married my whole life.

287
00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.340
And I can't imagine it any other way.

288
00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.700
They do everything together.

289
00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:31.140
Literally.

290
00:15:31.936 --> 00:15:33.640
Pop will go with Nan to the shops.

291
00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:37.512
He'll just sit on the couch outside KMart
and wait for her to do her thing.

292
00:15:37.672 --> 00:15:39.717
Has nothing to do. hates shopping.

293
00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:41.857
but he'll just sit there and wait.

294
00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:44.340
even before he had a phone to scroll on.

295
00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:50.500
I remember when I was about nine or ten.

296
00:15:50.767 --> 00:15:52.346
and I was sitting with him on the couch

297
00:15:52.406 --> 00:15:54.032
waiting for what seemed like hours.

298
00:15:54.953 --> 00:15:57.766
and I wanted to go to the bookstore. or

299
00:15:58.080 --> 00:15:59.880
to the shoe shop. or... I can't remember.

300
00:16:00.143 --> 00:16:03.211
And he said. no. he wouldn't let me go.

301
00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:05.763
This is where she knows we are.

302
00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:08.220
This is where we're going to wait.

303
00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:11.380
And now he does have a phone.

304
00:16:11.793 --> 00:16:13.746
And if he wanted to. he could wander off

305
00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:15.660
and do his own thing
and call her or something.

306
00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:17.498
But he doesn't.
He just sits there and tags her

307
00:16:17.523 --> 00:16:19.306
in a couple of dozen Facebook posts and

308
00:16:19.987 --> 00:16:21.280
dirty memes.

309
00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:26.270
And then she gets home later that night

310
00:16:26.295 --> 00:16:27.716
and hearts every single one of them

311
00:16:27.783 --> 00:16:29.300
to let him know that she's seen them.

312
00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:34.563
When they first met. they'd known each
other from around the traps

313
00:16:34.643 --> 00:16:35.750
but never talked.

314
00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.598
And then one day at a working bee.

315
00:16:38.754 --> 00:16:40.738
Pop was on lunch
and he was making a cup of tea.

316
00:16:40.798 --> 00:16:42.460
and he decided to make Nan one.

317
00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.300
and she said 'Thank you'

318
00:16:44.301 --> 00:16:45.341
and they started chatting.

319
00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.563
And by the end of the chat.
he'd asked her out on a date.

320
00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.100
And two months later. they were married.

321
00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.540
I don't think you've made
a cup of tea ever since. have you?

322
00:16:58.680 --> 00:16:59.680
No.

323
00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:02.460
Anyway.

324
00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:04.300
Say some words. mate.

325
00:17:06.253 --> 00:17:07.560
That was brilliant.

326
00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:10.120
Thank you all for coming.

327
00:17:12.102 --> 00:17:14.889
We don't usually celebrate this day.

328
00:17:14.969 --> 00:17:18.250
but 50 years is a long time.

329
00:17:18.284 --> 00:17:20.660
and we haven't had one of these since...

330
00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:24.185
Well. you know. it's...
yeah. it's been a while.

331
00:17:24.286 --> 00:17:25.286
um...

332
00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.843
Don't really know what to say.

333
00:17:30.146 --> 00:17:35.846
Oh. I guess I'd want to thank
my beautiful wife for putting up with me.

334
00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.400
Thank you. Lorna.

335
00:17:38.655 --> 00:17:41.481
Yeah. thank you for coming.

336
00:17:41.761 --> 00:17:43.248
It means a lot.

337
00:17:44.435 --> 00:17:48.498
So. um. have a drink for us. and

338
00:17:49.125 --> 00:17:51.280
if you can have one for Nora too...

339
00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:54.200
Yeah.

340
00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:56.520
Yeah. all right.

341
00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:58.403
Thank you.

342
00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:01.420
It was beautiful. darling.

343
00:18:11.931 --> 00:18:13.498
Okay. to the cake.

344
00:18:13.578 --> 00:18:15.151
Who wants to cut the cake?

345
00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:39.832
She looks so young.

346
00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:40.910
Well. she was young.

347
00:18:41.252 --> 00:18:42.956
Fifteen there. that 's them together.

348
00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:45.967
A week before her 16th birthday.

349
00:18:46.331 --> 00:18:47.833
Wasn't ideal. of course.

350
00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:49.807
But she was good with him.

351
00:18:50.734 --> 00:18:52.090
Very sweet.

352
00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:54.123
She had a beautiful voice. too.

353
00:18:54.222 --> 00:18:55.290
Really?

354
00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:58.053
Yeah. she sang in the choir.

355
00:18:58.077 --> 00:18:59.233
Loudest one there.

356
00:18:59.410 --> 00:19:01.446
Not a show-off. nothing like that. but

357
00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.266
you just knew who it was coming from.

358
00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:08.483
That's what I miss the most.

359
00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:12.999
I used to sit in a chair in the afternoon

360
00:19:13.023 --> 00:19:15.791
and hear her singing to him
when he was little.

361
00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:19.103
She's the only one
who could put him to sleep.

362
00:19:19.403 --> 00:19:20.550
Nobody else could.

363
00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:22.203
Problem child.

364
00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:24.403
No. no. no. no.

365
00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:26.457
He was just like her. though.

366
00:19:27.644 --> 00:19:29.470
They both picked up on the same things.

367
00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:34.136
Both tuned to the same frequency
I think. but. ah...

368
00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:38.171
he was better equipped for it. though.

369
00:19:42.070 --> 00:19:44.076
She just got lost somewhere.

370
00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.564
Don't know what went wrong.

371
00:19:51.846 --> 00:19:52.984
I wish I did.

372
00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:56.938
Oh. look. I'm sorry.

373
00:19:57.005 --> 00:19:59.200
This is not a
very good introduction to us. is it?

374
00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:00.270
What is?

375
00:20:00.534 --> 00:20:02.213
Yeah. right. yeah.

376
00:20:19.463 --> 00:20:21.383
That was a beautiful speech.

377
00:20:24.749 --> 00:20:26.189
I'm sorry about your mum.

378
00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:29.056
I had no idea.

379
00:20:30.603 --> 00:20:31.610
Yeah.

380
00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.384
She was 19 when she died.

381
00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:37.990
She was in and out of trouble

382
00:20:38.024 --> 00:20:40.017
and ended up dying of an overdose.

383
00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.743
Did Nan tell you she was fifteen
when she had me?

384
00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:46.643
She was a baby.

385
00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.030
I wouldn't go that far.

386
00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:49.743
Most 15-year-olds aren't out shoplifting

387
00:20:49.767 --> 00:20:51.104
and partying and doing drugs.

388
00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:53.717
whilst their baby is at home
with his stressed grandparents.

389
00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:56.463
Do you know what happened?

390
00:20:57.950 --> 00:21:01.087
She escaped rehab.
went missing for a couple of weeks.

391
00:21:01.341 --> 00:21:02.570
Not like in the movies.

392
00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:06.616
Just some random teen
camp out in the country.

393
00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:09.480
They took her there a couple of times.

394
00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:11.190
At first it was all good.

395
00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:15.062
She was apparently very serious about
being responsible for a while.

396
00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:17.142
And obviously that didn't stick.

397
00:21:18.856 --> 00:21:21.390
The second time.
I guess she just couldn't take it.

398
00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:26.235
Nan said she really
didn't want to go that time.

399
00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:30.843
And so she jumped a fence
and took off one night.

400
00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:44.052
They found her a couple of weeks later
in the city in some random house.

401
00:21:44.712 --> 00:21:47.105
They said she probably died
a few days earlier.

402
00:21:49.222 --> 00:21:50.255
That's awful.

403
00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:51.720
Yeah.

404
00:21:54.043 --> 00:21:55.110
Well...

405
00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:05.780
Yeah. Nan told me once she felt like a
sense of relief in the end.

406
00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:10.408
They'd been waiting years
for that phone call.

407
00:22:10.432 --> 00:22:12.570
years of constant anxiety and worry. and

408
00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:13.680
and then it was over.

409
00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:19.320
and she was sad. of course. but relieved.

410
00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:22.806
What about your grandpa?

411
00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:24.360
I don't know.

412
00:22:25.082 --> 00:22:27.316
I think we all
just remember her differently.

413
00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:11.708
I should go.

414
00:23:15.216 --> 00:23:16.470
I'd like you to stay.

415
00:23:34.658 --> 00:23:36.132
I just want you to be happy.
